allVulnerabilities.workaround:
The reporter of this vulnerability has suggested the following workarounds [here](https://github.com/EthanKim88/ethan-cve-disclosures/blob/main/CVE-2025-69873-ajv-ReDoS.md):
1. Avoid `$data` with the `pattern` keyword: If possible, use static patterns instead of dynamic ones. This eliminates the attack surface entirely.

2. Pre-validate patterns: If you must use dynamic patterns, validate them with `safe-regex` before they reach ajv:
```
const safeRegex = require('safe-regex');

function validateUserPattern(pattern) {
  if (!safeRegex(pattern)) {
    throw new Error('Potentially unsafe regex pattern');
  }
  return pattern;
}
```
3. Use the RE2 regex engine: Google's RE2 engine guarantees linear-time evaluation by disallowing backtracking entirely. ajv supports custom regex engines:
```
const RE2 = require('re2');
const ajv = new Ajv({
  $data: true,
  code: { regExp: (pattern, flags) => new RE2(pattern, flags) }
});
```

